[center][size=6][b]Heart of Magic Librarian[/b][/size]
[size=3]Spell learning as practice, on a tree grown from your own load order[/size]

A rebuild of [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/skyrimspecialedition/mods/171146]Heart of Magic - Spell Learning System[/url] by Dinkel Zombie.[/center]

[line]

[size=5][b]What it does[/b][/size]

Spells are no longer learned the moment you read a tome. Heart of Magic Librarian scans every spell in your load order - vanilla and mods alike - and grows a skill tree for each school, where each spell leads to related, stronger ones. You learn a spell by working toward it: casting its prerequisites, studying its tome, practising the school. Early on you can cast it weakened; at mastery it reaches full power.

No spell lists to maintain and no patches per spell mod: the tree is built from what is installed.

[line]

[size=5][b]What is different from Heart of Magic 2.5[/b][/size]

The idea is Dinkel Zombie's. Librarian keeps it and rebuilds everything around it, so that the tree is easier to read, quicker to use and smoother to handle - and so that it keeps your progress honest.

[size=4][b]Less grinding, more sense[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]Known Higher Spells.[/b] Already know a higher spell (bought, given, learned from another mod)? The spells below it open for a share of their XP (30% at Novice up to 80% at Master) instead of making you grind spells you have outgrown. Optionally the whole chain down to the root. On by default, tunable in Settings > Progression.
[*][b]Your cast always counts.[/b] Casting a spell that leads to your current target is always direct XP, whoever set the target - a tome, Papyrus or another mod.
[*][b]Trees that make sense in any language.[/b] Every spell is tagged with its element (fire, frost, shock, blood, water, holy, shadow...) and technique (summoning, siphon, curse...). Spells of one element grow on one branch, and a Korean, Chinese or Russian game gets the same tree as an English one.
[*][b]Paths between schools.[/b] A fire spell and a fire summon are linked across schools - an extra way in, never an extra requirement.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Everything you need, at a glance[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]A new spell card:[/b] icon, keyword chips, a description with the real numbers filled in, what it needs and what it opens. Click a chip and every spell with that keyword lights up.
[*][b]Preview on Hover:[/b] rest the cursor on a spell to read its card without clicking.
[*][b]Two spells with the same name[/b] (a mod's copy of a vanilla spell) show their plugin in the card's lists, so you always know which one is meant.
[*][b]Find Spell, school tabs and a Home button[/b] take you anywhere in the tree in one step.
[*][b]A language picker[/b] in Settings (13 languages, every text translated) - no more editing files, and the panel's text switches right away.
[/list]

[size=4][b]A panel that feels good to handle[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]Smooth.[/b] The tree is drawn once and reused: dragging draws only the newly uncovered edge, big repaints are spread over frames, hovering and XP gains no longer redraw the tree, and animations rest when you do. The goal is no visible frame drop in game.
[*][b]A camera that follows you.[/b] Clicking a spell glides to it (Center on Click, Zoom on Click, Focus Zoom, Rotate Wheel, Dim Others - each a setting).
[*][b]Clicks do what you meant.[/b] A click no longer nudges the tree. Esc first drops the selection, then closes the panel; Esc in Find Spell closes only the search.
[*][b]Tidy trees.[/b] Spells are moved off each other, off the lines and off the heart when a tree is built.
[*][b]Five designs[/b] - Classic (the old look), Modern Dark, Arcane (a parchment spellbook), Night Grimoire and Chalkboard - each with its own lines and fonts for every script. The Render popup turns off any moving part with one chip, or everything with "Still everything".
[/list]

[size=4][b]Progress you can trust[/b][/size]
[list]
[*]Tree prerequisites were forgotten after every save load in 2.5, so any spell could be learned from a tome for the rest of that session. Fixed.
[*]Early-learned (weakened) spells went back to full power on every load. Fixed.
[*]Spells no longer drop out of the tree after a load-order change.
[*]A possible crash when a cast finished learning a spell, fixed; progress is only changed on the game thread.
[*]The tree and settings are written safely with a backup copy, and saving settings no longer stalls the game.
[*]Simpler: one tree builder (Classic), no LLM or API key, no dead SkyrimNet scripts.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Scanning and tagging, built for patches[/b][/size]
In 2.5 the scan only gathered spells and the tree grouped them by name. In Librarian every scan also runs the [b]Librarian[/b], which gives each spell a set of tags and writes them to [i]spell_catalog.json[/i]:
[list]
[*][b]Four axes read from the records:[/b] school, tier, casting type and targeting.
[*][b]Elements and techniques from rules:[/b] fire, frost, shock, poison, arcane, blood, water, air, earth, holy, shadow... and summoning, siphon, curse, cloak, dispel... A rule reads what every load order has - magic effect keywords, archetypes, actor values, resistances - so no spell mod needs its own patch.
[*][b]Rules are data, not code.[/b] They are JSON files in [i]SKSE/Plugins/SpellLearning/librarian/[/i], merged by file name. Keyword frameworks (ADAR, KIT, NSV, OCF) get their own rule files that switch on when the framework is installed. Anyone can add a [i]90_user.json[/i] - by effect keyword, plugin name, archetype, or a single spell - without touching the DLL, and a tag outside the known vocabulary is refused with a warning instead of breaking the tree.
[*][b]Load-order-safe IDs.[/b] Spells are keyed by plugin and local ID ([i]Plugin.esp|0x000800[/i]), so a tree and its catalog survive a load-order change.
[*][b]More from the scan:[/b] which spells vampires cast (from NPCs, races, leveled lists and SPID distributions), editor IDs when powerofthree's Tweaks is installed, and a Papyrus call ([i]SpellLearning.RunScan()[/i]) that writes a tagged scan for other tools.
[/list]
The catalog is there for more than this tree: a perk mod, a patch or a spell browser can read the same tags instead of classifying spells again, and new spell mods can be supported with a small rule file.

[b]One honest limit:[/b] magic that is elemental only in concept - blood, water, wind and the like - usually has nothing in its records to say so. It deals plain health damage through a script, with no element keyword and no resistance. Those spells could not be caught by general rules and needed a separate hand-made patch, [i]80_manual.json[/i], which names them spell by spell for the spell mods it was made with. A spell mod of that kind that is not in it will show those spells without an element until someone adds a rule for it.

The full list is in the Changelog.

[line]

[size=5][b]Requirements[/b][/size]

[list]
[*]Skyrim Special Edition 1.5.97 or Anniversary Edition (not VR)
[*][url=https://skse.silverlock.org/]SKSE64[/url]
[*][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/skyrimspecialedition/mods/32444]Address Library for SKSE Plugins[/url]
[*][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/skyrimspecialedition/mods/148718]Prisma UI[/url]
[/list]

No ESP, no Python, no API key.

[b]Optional[/b]
[list]
[*][b]ISL-DESTified / Don't Eat Spell Tomes, Sit Down and Read, GTS Trait Rebalanced[/b] - the installer offers a merged compatibility patch (and picks it by itself when it finds ISL or DEST). Load Heart of Magic Librarian after them.
[*][b]An icon set[/b] (for example Kome's Inventory Tweaks, or Wheeler icon packs) for spell card icons.
[*][b]Keyword frameworks[/b] (ADAR, KIT, NSV, OCF) - extra tagging rules switch on by themselves.
[*][b]powerofthree's Tweaks[/b] with "Load EditorIDs" - lets the tree group spells by their editor IDs as well as their names.
[/list]

[size=5][b]Installation[/b][/size]

Install with your mod manager (MO2 or Vortex) and run the installer. Then in game: open the panel (F8 by default), scan your spells, and build the tree.

[b]Coming from Heart of Magic v2.5:[/b]
[list=1]
[*][b]Disable or remove Heart of Magic first[/b] - do not install over it. The files have the same names, and your mod manager will show this as a new mod at version 1.0.0.
[*]Your saves, settings and saved tree carry over.
[*][b]Scan and build your tree once more[/b] to get tags, school links and load-order-safe spell IDs.
[*]If you had entered an OpenRouter key, delete [i]SKSE/Plugins/SpellLearning/openrouter_config.json[/i] and the "llm" block in [i]config.json[/i] - nothing reads them any more.
[/list]

[size=5][b]Compatibility and notes[/b][/size]

[list]
[*]Works with any spell mod; nothing is hard-coded per mod.
[*]The spell tome hook is verified on SE 1.5.97 and AE 1.6.318 / 1.6.1170. On other versions, if [i]SpellLearning.log[/i] says "SpellTomeHook failed to install", tomes work the vanilla way and the rest of the mod still runs.
[*]Crash logs: the optional [b]Debug Symbols[/b] file puts function names into Crash Logger reports. Install it only if you want to send one.
[/list]

